<h2 id="two-decisions">Two different decisions</h2>

<dl>
  <dt>Changing the yearly increase</dt>
  <dd>This changes how fast the rates rise in future. It does not take away an amount already in payment. The same percentage applies to the full rates, whoever receives them, now or in future. Other parts of a payment rise by a different rule.</dd>
  <dt>Changing entitlement</dt>
  <dd>This changes who receives the State Pension, or how much, depending on their income or savings. It affects some people and not others.</dd>
</dl>

<p>Assessing a person's resources is also different from assessing their needs. A means test looks at income or savings. It does not look at what a person has to spend on rent, disability or care.</p>

<h2 id="public-finances">What the State Pension costs</h2>

<ul class="cards cards--three">
  <li>{{ m.stat("sp-spend-2024-25" | figure, "The latest full year with final figures.") }}</li>
  <li>{{ m.stat("sp-spend-2026-27" | figure, "The forecast for the current year.") }}</li>
  <li>{{ m.stat("sp-share-gdp-2025-26" | figure, "The size of the bill compared with the whole economy.") }}</li>
</ul>

{{ m.dataTable("sp-spending-series") }}

{% call m.note("State Pension spending is not all spending on older people", 3) %}
<p>DWP spent {{ m.f("pensioner-benefits-2024-25") }} in 2024/25 on all benefits for pensioners, of which the State Pension was {{ m.f("sp-spend-2024-25") }}. Neither figure includes the NHS or social care.</p>
<p>Official sources give slightly different figures for the same year. The DWP tables give {{ m.f("sp-spend-2024-25") }}. The account of the National Insurance Fund gives {{ m.f("nif-state-pension-2024-25") }}, on a cash basis. They differ in accounting basis and coverage. This site uses the DWP tables for spending and says so each time.</p>
{% endcall %}

<h3>The long-term projection</h3>

{{ m.dataTable("sp-longrun") }}

<p>The Office for Budget Responsibility projects a rise of {{ m.f("sp-rise-gdp-total") }} between 2030-31 and 2075-76. It attributes {{ m.f("tl-share-of-rise") }} of that to the triple lock. Most of the rest comes from an older population.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("tl-share-of-rise" | figure) }}

<p>The projection reaches {{ m.f("sp-share-gdp-2075-76") }} in 2075-76. It assumes the triple lock adds {{ m.f("tl-premium-obr") }} percentage points a year to earnings growth on average. {{ ("obr-tl-assumed" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("obr-tl-assumed") }}</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("sp-share-gdp-2075-76" | figure) }}

<p>Four things drive the bill, and only one of them is the yearly increase.</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The number of pensioners.</strong> The number of people of pensionable age in the UK is projected to rise from {{ m.f("pop-pensionable-2024") }} in 2024 to {{ m.f("pop-pensionable-2034") }} in 2034.</li>
  <li><strong>State Pension age.</strong> A higher age means fewer recipients. The fall in recipients forecast for 2027/28 in the table above comes with the rise to 67.</li>
  <li><strong>Entitlement.</strong> More people on the new State Pension, and fewer on the older system, changes the average amount.</li>
  <li><strong>The yearly increase.</strong> The subject of the next section.</li>
</ul>
{{ m.sourceNote("pop-pensionable-2034" | figure) }}

<p>The balance between pensioners and people of working age is measured in several ways. On the measure used by the Office for National Statistics there were {{ m.f("oadr-2024") }} people of pensionable age for every 1,000 of working age in 2024, projected to be {{ m.f("oadr-2049") }} in 2049. Measures that use a fixed age, such as 65, show a steeper change, because they do not move with State Pension age.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("oadr-2049" | figure) }}

<p>Future numbers of workers depend partly on migration. {{ m.sisterLink("migration" | link) }} explains how migration is measured. It does not estimate the effect on pension costs, and neither does this site.</p>

<p>Pensioners also pay tax. {{ m.f("pensioner-taxpayers") }} people above State Pension age paid Income Tax in 2023 to 2024. {{ m.c("ni-stops-at-spa") }}</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("pensioner-taxpayers" | figure) }}

<p>For the difference between what the government spends or borrows in a year and the debt it has built up, see {{ m.sisterLink("debt-vs-deficit" | link) }}.</p>

<h2 id="cost-and-saving">What the triple lock has cost, and what a change would save</h2>

<p>Three different amounts are often confused.</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>The whole bill.</strong> {{ m.f("sp-spend-2026-27") }} in 2026/27, above.</li>
  <li><strong>The cost of the triple lock so far.</strong> How much higher spending is than it would have been under a different rule. It depends on which rule is used for comparison.</li>
  <li><strong>The saving from a change.</strong> How much lower future spending would be. It depends on the new rule, the start date and the year measured.</li>
</ol>

<h3>The cost so far: a difference, not a bill</h3>

