<div class="source"><p>Estimates and projections, not forecasts of what will happen. None describes a cut to pensions already in payment. The sources do not say so in those words: this is our reading of what each one models. Checked against the sources on {{ ("saving-earnings-2075" | figure).checked_date | timeTag | safe }}.</p></div>

<p>The pattern is consistent across sources. In the next few years the saving is small compared with the bill. Over decades it becomes large. The Institute for Fiscal Studies puts the cost of keeping the triple lock in 2050 at between {{ m.f("ifs-tl-cost-2050-low") }} and {{ m.f("ifs-tl-cost-2050-high") }} a year, a wide range because the result depends on how volatile prices and earnings turn out to be.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("ifs-tl-cost-2050-high" | figure) }}

<p>Spending would still rise without the triple lock. The Institute for Fiscal Studies says: “{{ ("ifs-rise-without-tl" | figure).passage }}”.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("ifs-rise-without-tl" | figure) }}

{% call m.unavailable("costings for a double lock and for means-testing", 3) %}
<p>{{ ("no-costed-double-lock" | claim).statement }} {{ ("no-costed-means-test" | claim).statement }} We have not made our own estimates. A calculation that multiplied one person's difference by the number of pensioners would be wrong, because people receive different amounts and because tax and benefits change the result.</p>
{% endcall %}

<p>To see the State Pension beside other spending and taxes, the {{ m.sisterLink("budget-simulator" | link) }} lets you change each one. It is a simplified teaching model, not an official costing of any pension reform, and its State Pension figure is a forecast for an earlier year than the figures on this page. To put a figure in billions into scale, there is the {{ m.sisterLink("big-numbers" | link) }}. An amount per person from that tool is simple division. It is not what anyone pays or receives.</p>

<h2 id="who">Who receives what: incomes, wealth and poverty</h2>

<p>An average pensioner does not exist. The evidence below is about the spread.</p>

<h3>Income</h3>

{{ m.dataTable("income-fifths") }}

<p>The State Pension matters most to people with the lowest incomes, and it still matters to those with the highest. It makes up {{ m.f("sp-share-income-poorest") }} of income for the poorest fifth of pensioners and {{ m.f("sp-share-income-richest") }} for the richest fifth.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("sp-share-income-poorest" | figure) }}

<p>On this page, "low income" and "high income" mean a position in these distributions, unless a source's own definition is given.</p>

<h3>Poverty and deprivation</h3>

<ul class="cards cards--three">
  <li>{{ m.stat("low-income-ahc" | figure, "After rent, mortgage interest and similar costs are taken off.") }}</li>
  <li>{{ m.stat("low-income-bhc" | figure, "Before housing costs are taken off.") }}</li>
  <li>{{ m.stat("material-deprivation" | figure, "A measure of going without, not of income.") }}</li>
</ul>

<p>Fewer pensioners are in low income after housing costs than before, because most own their home outright. {{ m.c("hbai-owners") }} For a pensioner who rents, housing costs work the other way.</p>

<h3>Housing and wealth</h3>

<p>Of households headed by someone aged 65 or over, {{ m.f("tenure-own-outright") }} own outright, {{ m.f("tenure-mortgage") }} are buying with a mortgage, {{ m.f("tenure-social-rent") }} rent from a social landlord and {{ m.f("tenure-private-rent") }} rent privately.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("tenure-own-outright" | figure) }}

{{ m.dataTable("wealth-bands") }}

<p>Wealth is spread more unevenly than income. Among single households over State Pension age, a quarter have total wealth below {{ m.f("wealth-single-p25") }} and a quarter have more than {{ m.f("wealth-single-p75") }}.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("wealth-single-p25" | figure) }}

{% call m.note("Income, wealth and need are three different things", 4) %}
<p>A person can have a valuable home and a low income. A home cannot pay a bill unless it is sold or borrowed against. A person can have a high income and high costs, for example for care. People with high income and people with high wealth are not the same group, and neither measure shows what a person needs.</p>
{% endcall %}

<h3>Differences between men and women</h3>

<p>Among people aged 65 to 74, the median private pension wealth of men is {{ m.f("pension-wealth-men-65-74") }} and of women is {{ m.f("pension-wealth-women-65-74") }}, counting people with none. Women on the older State Pension system also receive less on average than men. <a href="/state-pension/#what-people-receive">What people actually receive</a>. A person with little private pension depends more on the State Pension, and so is more exposed to a change in it.</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("pension-wealth-women-65-74" | figure) }}

<h3>Disability and caring</h3>

<p>{{ m.f("disabled-share-spa") }} of adults of State Pension age report a disability. {{ ("disability-income-artefact" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("disability-income-artefact") }}</p>
{{ m.sourceNote("disabled-share-spa" | figure) }}

{% call m.unavailable("some breakdowns", 3) %}
<p>We did not find published figures for the incomes of pensioners who are carers, for wealth by type among people aged 65 and over, or for the number of people who would gain or lose under each reform. We have not filled these gaps with examples. Example households can show how a rule works. They cannot show how many people are affected.</p>
{% endcall %}

<h2 id="increases">Options for the yearly increase</h2>

<p>Each option is described the same way. The law sets a minimum of earnings for any option: a rule that pays less than earnings growth would need a change in the law. {{ m.c("legal-earnings-floor") }}</p>

<div class="option-grid">
  <section class="option" aria-labelledby="opt-triple">
    <h3 id="opt-triple">Keep the triple lock</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>The rule</dt><dd>The highest of prices, earnings and 2.5%.</dd>
      <dt>Effect on the pension</dt><dd>It rises at least as fast as prices and as earnings, every year. Over time it rises faster than both. The level in future is uncertain: {{ ("ifs-range-2050" | claim).passage }}. {{ m.c("ifs-range-2050") }}</dd>
      <dt>Who is affected</dt><dd>Current pensioners, and future ones more so, because increases build up over time. {{ m.c("ppi-future-workers") }} People who live longer gain more. {{ m.c("ifs-longevity") }}</dd>
      <dt>Public finances</dt><dd>The highest cost of the four rules, and the least predictable.</dd>
      <dt>A judgement, not a finding</dt><dd>Whether the pension should rise as a share of earnings, and how far.</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>
  <section class="option" aria-labelledby="opt-double">
    <h3 id="opt-double">Double lock: prices or earnings</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>The rule</dt><dd>The higher of prices and earnings. The 2.5% minimum is removed.</dd>
      <dt>Effect on the pension</dt><dd>The same as the triple lock in any year when prices or earnings rise by 2.5% or more. Lower in other years. It still rises faster than both prices and earnings over time, because it takes the higher each year.</dd>
      <dt>Who is affected</dt><dd>The same people, by less. Modelling from 2018 put the effect on pensioner poverty in 2050 at {{ "ppi-poverty-double-lock" | figShown }} more people than under the triple lock. {{ m.cite(("ppi-poverty-earnings-link" | figure).source) }}.</dd>
      <dt>Public finances</dt><dd>{{ ("no-costed-double-lock" | claim).statement }}</dd>
      <dt>A precedent</dt><dd>{{ ("double-lock-2017" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("double-lock-2017") }}</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>
  <section class="option" aria-labelledby="opt-earnings">
    <h3 id="opt-earnings">Earnings link</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>The rule</dt><dd>The rise in average earnings. This is the minimum the law already requires.</dd>
      <dt>Effect on the pension</dt><dd>It keeps its value compared with pay. In a year when prices rise faster than pay, it loses buying power.</dd>
      <dt>Who is affected</dt><dd>Modelling from 2018 put the number of extra pensioners in poverty in 2050 at {{ "ppi-poverty-earnings-link" | figShown }}, compared with the triple lock. {{ m.sourceNote("ppi-poverty-earnings-link" | figure) }}</dd>
      <dt>Public finances</dt><dd>Small savings at first, large over decades. See the table above.</dd>
      <dt>A judgement, not a finding</dt><dd>Whether a year of lost buying power is acceptable if the pension keeps pace with pay over time.</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>
  <section class="option" aria-labelledby="opt-prices">
    <h3 id="opt-prices">Prices link</h3>
    <dl>
      <dt>The rule</dt><dd>The rise in the Consumer Prices Index.</dd>
      <dt>Effect on the pension</dt><dd>It keeps its buying power against the official index. When pay rises faster than prices, which is the usual long-run pattern, it falls further behind pay each year.</dd>
      <dt>Who is affected</dt><dd>Future pensioners most, because the gap with earnings grows with time.</dd>
      <dt>Public finances</dt><dd>The largest saving of the four. See the table above.</dd>
      <dt>The law</dt><dd>It would pay less than the present legal minimum in most years, so the law would have to change. From 1980 to 2010 the law required only a prices link. {{ m.c("earnings-link-1980") }}</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>
</div>

<h3 id="smoothed">A fifth option, explained but not in the calculator: a smoothed earnings link</h3>

<p>{{ ("smoothed-link-definition" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("smoothed-link-definition") }} It is the rule proposed, in different forms, by three research bodies.</p>
<ul>
  <li>{{ ("ifs-guarantee-1" | claim).statement }} {{ ("ifs-guarantee-2" | claim).statement }} {{ ("ifs-not-immediately" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("ifs-guarantee-1") }}</li>
  <li>{{ ("rf-smoothed-recommendation" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("rf-smoothed-recommendation") }}</li>
  <li>{{ ("tbi-proposal" | claim).statement }} {{ m.c("tbi-proposal") }}</li>
</ul>
<p>These are recommendations. They rest on evidence and on a view about what the pension should be worth.</p>

{% call m.note("Why this rule is not in the calculator", 4) %}
<p>The result of a smoothed link depends on details that the proposals set differently: the target level, how the pension catches up after a year of price protection, and over how long. Until those are specified and checked, a calculation would be our invention. The rule is described here and left out of the comparison below.</p>
{% endcall %}

<p>A freeze, with no increase at all, is not among the options. We found no current proposal for one.</p>

<section class="tool" aria-labelledby="compare">
  <h2 id="compare">Try it: compare the rules</h2>

  <p>This tool applies four rules to the same starting amount and the same yearly figures for prices and earnings. It shows where the rules differ and where they do not.</p>

  {% call m.note("What this tool does and does not show", 3) %}
  <ul>
    <li>It models <strong>one full rate</strong>, weekly and <strong>before tax</strong>. It is not your entitlement.</li>
    <li>The yearly figures are <strong>illustrations</strong>. They are not forecasts and have no probability.</li>
    <li>It leaves out Income Tax, Pension Credit and every other benefit. A difference shown here is not a difference in money to spend.</li>
    <li>It cannot be used to work out national savings.</li>
    <li>The tool does not send or store anything you enter.</li>
  </ul>
  {% endcall %}

  <div class="no-js-only">
    {% call m.note("The calculator needs JavaScript", 3) %}
    <p>The form is not available, because JavaScript is off or did not load. The result below shows the first scenario.</p>
    {% endcall %}
  </div>

  <form id="compare-form" class="js-only" novalidate>
    <fieldset>
